WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:06.590
<v Speaker 1>If you want to become a master painter, you do not just trace over a master's brushstrokes.

2
00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:09.710
<v Speaker 1>Tracing might yield A flawless copy of a masterpiece.

3
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:14.350
<v Speaker 1>You might match the curves, the shading, the exact geometric proportions of the original.

4
00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:16.590
<v Speaker 1>But tracing does not make you an artist.

5
00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.350
<v Speaker 1>It makes you a human stencil.

6
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:19.470
<v Speaker 2>I see the distinction.

7
00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:24.190
<v Speaker 1>You are producing the gesture without possessing the vision that birthed the gesture.

8
00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:26.910
<v Speaker 1>Today we are exploring a historical argument

9
00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:31.070
<v Speaker 1>that a vast number of people make this exact mistake with their spiritual lives.

10
00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:32.270
<v Speaker 1>Welcome.

11
00:00:32.270 --> 00:00:34.510
<v Speaker 1>I am so glad you are here with us for this deep dive.

12
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.630
<v Speaker 1>Settle in, clear your mind, and make yourself comfortable.

13
00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:41.430
<v Speaker 1>We are stepping into a very deliberate, unhurried space today.

14
00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:52.350
<v Speaker 1>We are opening Reza Shah Kazemi's book, Spiritual Ethos, to look at a text that asks us to reconsider the very architecture of spiritual life by focusing on a single monumental figure.

15
00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:55.390
<v Speaker 1>The thesis we are going to explore is profound.

16
00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.030
<v Speaker 1>and it must be stated exactly as the text presents it.

17
00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:03.870
<v Speaker 1>Ali is the Prophet's own soul, the gate through which the Quran and the prophetic nature became a living human path.

18
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:09.470
<v Speaker 2>Our mission for this exploration is to fundamentally redefine what it truly means to follow a spiritual exemplar.

19
00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:16.990
<v Speaker 2>In our modern discourse, the concept of following has been, well, it has been flattened into something procedural and superficial.

20
00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:17.630
<v Speaker 1>It has.

21
00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:26.110
<v Speaker 2>We are going to explore the idea that following the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, does not mean merely copying his outward acts.

22
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.230
<v Speaker 2>It means inwardly assimilating his very substance.

23
00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:35.390
<v Speaker 1>To understand what that assimilation actually looks like, we have to start by setting a historical stage.

24
00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:40.230
<v Speaker 1>We need to go back to a very specific room at a very specific moment in time.

25
00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.590
<v Speaker 1>The crisis begins at a clan feast.

26
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:43.710
<v Speaker 2>Picture the gathering.

27
00:01:43.790 --> 00:01:46.350
<v Speaker 2>It is a feast of the Hashimite clan in Mecca.

28
00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:50.750
<v Speaker 2>In this room sit the grown, proud leaders of the tribal structure.

29
00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:52.110
<v Speaker 1>The heavyweights of the society.

30
00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:53.390
<v Speaker 2>Precisely those men.

31
00:01:53.710 --> 00:02:01.150
<v Speaker 2>These are men of immense social standing, deeply steeped in the heavy responsibilities, the economic realities, and the fierce politics of their time.

32
00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.150
<v Speaker 2>They have been summoned by the Prophet.

33
00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:08.350
<v Speaker 2>He stands before them and asks a question that will quite literally alter the course of human history.

34
00:02:08.590 --> 00:02:10.430
<v Speaker 1>Let me set up the sheer gravity of this.

35
00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:10.830
<v Speaker 2>Please do.

36
00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:15.870
<v Speaker 1>He looks at these seasoned leaders and asks, who among you will help me in this mission?

37
00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:19.070
<v Speaker 1>Who will be my brother, my executor, and my successor?

38
00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:21.790
<v Speaker 1>We must examine the anatomy of what happens next.

39
00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:28.590
<v Speaker 1>The Prophet asks this monumental question and the response from the most powerful men in the room is a heavy, suffocating silence.

40
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:30.910
<v Speaker 2>A profound, paralyzing silence.

41
00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:34.670
<v Speaker 2>These seasoned men meet this question by doing absolutely nothing.

42
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:35.790
<v Speaker 2>They're calculating.

43
00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:39.590
<v Speaker 2>We must understand the geopolitical reality of Mecca at that moment.

44
00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:41.070
<v Speaker 1>The stakes were unimaginably high.

45
00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:41.950
<v Speaker 2>They were.

46
00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:47.150
<v Speaker 2>Mecca's entire economy and social hierarchy are built upon the idol worship at the sanctuary.

47
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:52.350
<v Speaker 2>The prophet's message of absolute monotheism threatens to shatter that economic lifeblood.

48
00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:55.230
<v Speaker 2>It threatens to overturn the entire social order.

49
00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.910
<v Speaker 1>So to align with this message is to invite absolute ruin.

50
00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.110
<v Speaker 2>To stand up in that room is to volunteer

51
00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:03.790
<v Speaker 2>exile, economic devastation, and likely war.

52
00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.350
<v Speaker 2>So they weigh the costs, they hesitate.

53
00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:11.150
<v Speaker 1>Think about the last time you were in a room where speaking the truth carried a massive social cost.

54
00:03:11.630 --> 00:03:17.150
<v Speaker 1>That paralyzing silence is human nature operating at its most basic self-preserving level.

55
00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:22.110
<v Speaker 1>But against the backdrop of that total silence, one voice speaks up.

56
00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:23.390
<v Speaker 1>One single voice.

57
00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:24.110
<v Speaker 2>It is the voice.

58
00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:26.510
<v Speaker 1>Of a boy, perhaps 13 or 14 years old.

59
00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:28.430
<v Speaker 1>He stands up alone to answer.

60
00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:32.190
<v Speaker 1>This boy is Ali, peace be upon him.

61
00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:39.150
<v Speaker 2>A young teenager surrounded by the most powerful men in his world, and he is the singular individual who does not pause to calculate the cost.

62
00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:47.470
<v Speaker 2>That instinct to answer when grown men hesitated, that immediate unflinching readiness, it forms the miniature shape of his entire life.

63
00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:48.590
<v Speaker 1>It becomes his signature.

64
00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:50.350
<v Speaker 2>It is the signature of his character.

65
00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:58.110
<v Speaker 2>And to speak of Ali, to study this singular flakr of earliest Islam is to speak of the quintessential spirituality of the Islamic tradition itself.

66
00:03:58.430 --> 00:03:59.990
<v Speaker 2>The entire edifice of Islamic

67
00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:06.190
<v Speaker 2>spirituality flows from 2 fundamental sources, the revelation of the Quran and the inspired example of the prophet.

68
00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.910
<v Speaker 1>How is this different from ordinary imitation?

69
00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:13.870
<v Speaker 1>When we talk about following an example, we usually mean someone we watch and try to mimic.

70
00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:17.790
<v Speaker 1>We catalog their actions and try to reproduce them in our own lives.

71
00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:20.190
<v Speaker 2>That is the core crisis we have to navigate here.

72
00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:31.710
<v Speaker 2>When the perdition speaks of the prophet's Sunnah, his living example, modern reductionism views it exactly as you just described, as a set of gestures to be codified, cataloged, and copied.

73
00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.310
<v Speaker 1>Like checking boxes on a rubric.

74
00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:34.110
<v Speaker 2>Just like that.

75
00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:43.790
<v Speaker 2>But we must draw our attention to a specific verse in the Quran which addresses the prophet directly, stating, Verily, thou art of a tremendous nature.

76
00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:46.030
<v Speaker 2>That is chapter 68, verse 4.

77
00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:46.990
<v Speaker 1>A tremendous nature.

78
00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:50.510
<v Speaker 2>That tremendous nature is the spiritual substance of prophetic perfection.

79
00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:55.790
<v Speaker 2>The words and deeds of the prophet are merely the outward expressions of that inward substance.

80
00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:57.150
<v Speaker 2>They do not exhaust it.

81
00:04:57.470 --> 00:05:00.990
<v Speaker 2>Following in this spiritual context is not mimicking gestures.

82
00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:07.150
<v Speaker 2>It is the arduous, transformative process of assimilating that inward, tremendous nature into your own being.

83
00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:12.750
<v Speaker 1>So we are talking about a failed reading of what it means to be a follower, the immense temptation of outward imitation.

84
00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:16.110
<v Speaker 2>It is a profound and incredibly common pitfall.

85
00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:21.270
<v Speaker 2>A person might copy the outward forms of the prophet's conduct with flawless exactitude.

86
00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:22.670
<v Speaker 1>Down to the smallest detail.

87
00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:30.030
<v Speaker 2>They might perfect the physical gestures of prayer, the daily routines of eating and sleeping, the precise speech patterns.

88
00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:36.110
<v Speaker 2>They might look entirely orthodox, yet spiritually they could remain entirely on the outside.

89
00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:37.710
<v Speaker 1>Like the tracing we discussed earlier.

90
00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.870
<v Speaker 2>They are tracing the lines of the masterpiece.

91
00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:46.190
<v Speaker 2>They are imitating the gesture without possessing the internal reality, the spiritual state that birthed the gesture.

92
00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:53.070
<v Speaker 1>I don't buy that yet because we live in an era that fundamentally believes in the power of habituation.

93
00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.670
<v Speaker 1>Modern psychology preaches fake it till you make it.

94
00:05:57.470 --> 00:06:04.910
<v Speaker 1>The concept of neuroplasticity suggests that outward, repetitive physical actions actually rewire your internal chemistry over time.

95
00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:06.190
<v Speaker 2>I hear that argument often.

96
00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:19.230
<v Speaker 1>If a person flawlessly replicates a perfect outward action over and over, if they pray with the exact physical movements, if they give to charity exactly as instructed, if they police their speech meticulously,

97
00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:25.470
<v Speaker 1>Wouldn't that intense physical discipline eventually cultivate a perfect internal state of being organically?

98
00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:28.190
<v Speaker 1>Why wouldn't the outside eventually shape the inside?

99
00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:32.190
<v Speaker 2>The temptation of the modern procedural mind is to want a mechanism.

100
00:06:32.590 --> 00:06:38.190
<v Speaker 2>We want a guarantee that if we input the correct physical data, the machine will output spiritual perfection.

101
00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:42.110
<v Speaker 2>But spiritual reality operates on an entirely different physics.

102
00:06:42.110 --> 00:06:43.310
<v Speaker 1>Different physics entirely.

103
00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:46.430
<v Speaker 2>The outward shaping the inside is habituation, yes.

104
00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:54.190
<v Speaker 2>You can habituate a person to be polite, you can habituate a person to be calm, but you cannot habituate a person into possessing A tremendous nature.

105
00:06:54.670 --> 00:07:01.070
<v Speaker 2>Genuine spiritual right action must flow organically from within rather than being synthetically imposed from without.

106
00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:02.910
<v Speaker 1>Okay, I am starting to see the gap there.

107
00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:07.630
<v Speaker 2>Think of the difference between a puppet whose limbs are moved perfectly by external strings

108
00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:13.390
<v Speaker 2>living creature whose movements are the natural expression of its own internal life force.

109
00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:24.750
<v Speaker 2>There is a profound categorical difference between a follower who merely trails behind an example perpetually striving to match a stencil and a follower who has been inwardly remade by it.

110
00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:28.270
<v Speaker 1>So one is being pulled and the other is moving under its own power.

111
00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:30.110
<v Speaker 2>Ali represents the second kind.

112
00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:33.710
<v Speaker 2>The prophetic substance genuinely took up residence within him.

113
00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:35.710
<v Speaker 2>He did not just study the example.

114
00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:38.150
<v Speaker 2>He became an extension of the reality behind it.

115
00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:40.430
<v Speaker 1>Because he possessed the life force itself.

116
00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:47.230
<v Speaker 2>To contextualize the mechanics of how this is even possible, we must look at another Quranic verse, chapter 33, verse 6.

117
00:07:47.230 --> 00:07:51.710
<v Speaker 2>It states, the prophet is closer to the believers than they are to themselves.

118
00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:54.350
<v Speaker 2>This is a staggering ontological claim.

119
00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:56.470
<v Speaker 1>Closer to the believers than they are to themselves.

120
00:07:56.470 --> 00:08:01.710
<v Speaker 2>Well, it reveals A mysterious affinity that transcends time, physical space, and biological lineage.

121
00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:03.150
<v Speaker 1>Let us linger on that.

122
00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:06.590
<v Speaker 1>How can another being be closer to me than my own self?

123
00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:09.630
<v Speaker 1>My own self is the very center of my perception.

124
00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:12.750
<v Speaker 1>I mean, there's nothing closer to me than my own ego.

125
00:08:12.830 --> 00:08:15.870
<v Speaker 2>It forces us to ask what the self actually is.

126
00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:22.670
<v Speaker 2>If your self is just your ego, your calculated desires, the part of you that hesitates at a clan feast, then yes, you are the center of that.

127
00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:29.230
<v Speaker 2>But the tradition teaches that beneath the ego is the primordial soul, the divine breath.

128
00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:30.110
<v Speaker 1>The deepest core.

129
00:08:30.110 --> 00:08:32.750
<v Speaker 2>The prophetic light is the origin of that purity.

130
00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:43.070
<v Speaker 2>Therefore, the awakened soul intuits the prophetic essence not as something foreign to be studied from afar, but as the deepest, truest core of its own identity.

131
00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:45.310
<v Speaker 2>It's a closeness of essence.

132
00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:53.310
<v Speaker 1>If the Prophet is closer to every awakened believer than they are to themselves, we have to look closely at the language the Prophet used when speaking specifically about Ali.

133
00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:57.790
<v Speaker 1>There are three key sayings we need to walk through that build this interiorization thesis.

134
00:08:57.870 --> 00:08:59.710
<v Speaker 2>The 3 sayings form progression.

135
00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:03.390
<v Speaker 1>The first is a saying where the Prophet declares that Ali is as my own soul.

136
00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:05.950
<v Speaker 2>We must weigh that phrase very slowly.

137
00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:12.670
<v Speaker 2>On the surface, an historian or a cynic could read it merely as a statement of deep familial affection.

138
00:09:13.150 --> 00:09:13.950
<v Speaker 2>There were cousins.

139
00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:15.310
<v Speaker 2>They lived in one household.

140
00:09:15.550 --> 00:09:17.790
<v Speaker 2>The prophet was the older figure who raised him.

141
00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:19.790
<v Speaker 1>It sounds like a father speaking of a son.

142
00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:21.350
<v Speaker 2>It does, but this.

143
00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:24.030
<v Speaker 2>reaches far past biographical affection.

144
00:09:24.430 --> 00:09:33.390
<v Speaker 2>By affirming that Ali is as my own soul, the Prophet is affirming an ultimate realized identity between the sanctified human soul and the prophetic nature.

145
00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:36.110
<v Speaker 1>It is bridging the human and the prophetic.

146
00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:49.230
<v Speaker 2>It is a highly particular bond between two specific historical individuals, yes, but it serves to reveal a universal spiritual principle about what the human soul is capable of becoming when it is fully turned toward its source.

147
00:09:49.670 --> 00:09:53.070
<v Speaker 1>The second saying pushes this even further into the realm of the extraordinary.

148
00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:55.750
<v Speaker 1>The prophet said, you are from me and I am from you.

149
00:09:55.830 --> 00:09:57.070
<v Speaker 2>The second-half of that is the key.

150
00:09:57.310 --> 00:10:00.510
<v Speaker 1>Okay, let's unpack this, because the first half makes complete sense.

151
00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:09.310
<v Speaker 1>A pupil being from a teacher or a younger man raised by a master being from that master fits all standard hierarchies of influence.

152
00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:14.750
<v Speaker 1>But the prophet saying, and I am from you, completely defies that hierarchy.

153
00:10:14.750 --> 00:10:17.470
<v Speaker 2>It entirely subverts the standard flow of authority.

154
00:10:17.950 --> 00:10:23.150
<v Speaker 2>For the prophet to say, and I am from you, points to something much deeper than a lineage of influence.

155
00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:26.070
<v Speaker 2>It points to a profound mutual indwelling.

156
00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:26.910
<v Speaker 1>Mutual indwelling.

157
00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:33.150
<v Speaker 2>It implies that what the prophet carried and what Ali carried were not two separate substances operating in tandem.

158
00:10:33.630 --> 00:10:37.390
<v Speaker 2>They were one shared reality expressed through two distinct persons.

159
00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:43.470
<v Speaker 2>The Prophet is acknowledging a communion that cannot be reduced to a mere teacher and student dynamic.

160
00:10:43.950 --> 00:10:46.750
<v Speaker 2>Consider our first governing analogy here, the mirror.

161
00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:48.910
<v Speaker 1>Explain the mechanics of the mirror in this context.

162
00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:56.270
<v Speaker 2>Think of ancient metallurgy, where a mirror was not made of glass, but of metal that had to be painstakingly polished of all rust and oxidation.

163
00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:02.190
<v Speaker 2>The rust represents the ego, the individual desires, the calculations that cloud human perception.

164
00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:04.590
<v Speaker 1>The calculations we saw from the men at the feast.

165
00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:09.630
<v Speaker 2>Ali's readiness at 13 years old shows a mirror entirely devoid of rust.

166
00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:17.070
<v Speaker 2>Because his soul was perfectly polished of any obscuring ego, he could perfectly reflect the Prophet's own substance.

167
00:11:17.590 --> 00:11:19.470
<v Speaker 1>He receives it without altering it.

168
00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:24.110
<v Speaker 2>He receives the light wholly while adding absolutely nothing of his own distortion to it.

169
00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:32.270
<v Speaker 2>The reflection is so perfect, so pristine, that the source, the prophet, sees his own essence entirely within the reflection.

170
00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:34.070
<v Speaker 2>Hence, I am from you.

171
00:11:34.750 --> 00:11:37.310
<v Speaker 2>The light recognizes itself in the flawless mirror.

172
00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:44.110
<v Speaker 1>That brings us to the third saying, which shifts the focus slightly from the purse of the prophet to the revelation itself.

173
00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:47.790
<v Speaker 1>The prophet said, Ali is with the Quran and the Quran is with Ali.

174
00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:51.230
<v Speaker 1>They will not separate from each other until they return to me at the paradisal pool.

175
00:11:51.390 --> 00:11:53.310
<v Speaker 2>This is a monumental declaration.

176
00:11:53.310 --> 00:11:54.270
<v Speaker 1>That sounds like wordplay.

177
00:11:54.470 --> 00:12:02.350
<v Speaker 1>It is one thing to perfectly reflect a human being, even a perfect human being, but what does it actually mean for a human being to be with a text until the end of time?

178
00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:04.430
<v Speaker 1>Books are ink and paper.

179
00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:05.950
<v Speaker 1>people are flesh and blood.

180
00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:10.510
<v Speaker 1>You can study a text, you can memorize a text, but how can you mutually indwell with a book?

181
00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:17.470
<v Speaker 2>It demands that we stop viewing the Revelation as an inert physical object, mere ink and paper.

182
00:12:18.030 --> 00:12:21.550
<v Speaker 2>We must understand it as a living, breathing spiritual force.

183
00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:26.510
<v Speaker 2>This third saying is the ultimate statement of absolute interiorization.

184
00:12:26.670 --> 00:12:28.590
<v Speaker 1>Interiorization of the text itself.

185
00:12:28.950 --> 00:12:31.550
<v Speaker 2>Ali did not just memorize the vocabulary of the text.

186
00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:35.310
<v Speaker 2>He did not merely study its legal rulings or historical narratives.

187
00:12:35.710 --> 00:12:41.470
<v Speaker 2>He became the book's inseparable companion by making its inner reality the very substance of his own soul.

188
00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:44.910
<v Speaker 2>He lives within its meaning and its meaning lives within him.

189
00:12:44.910 --> 00:12:46.630
<v Speaker 1>He became the walking embodiment of it.

190
00:12:46.630 --> 00:12:49.630
<v Speaker 2>He became the walking, breathing embodiment of the divine word.

191
00:12:50.230 --> 00:12:59.470
<v Speaker 2>When we synthesize these three sayings, we see that the twin pillars of Islamic spirituality, the Quran and the Prophet, were not left as external objects of study.

192
00:12:59.550 --> 00:13:02.270
<v Speaker 2>They were fully absorbed into a living human being.

193
00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:05.230
<v Speaker 1>We've been talking entirely about the inward reality so far.

194
00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:09.870
<v Speaker 1>The inner assimilation, the mutual indwelling, the polishing of the mirror of the soul.

195
00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:13.910
<v Speaker 1>But a soul cannot contain an ocean without it breaching the banks.

196
00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:15.070
<v Speaker 2>It has to spill over.

197
00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:22.030
